WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.377
Environmental conditions can become harder to recognize after sunset.

00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:11.256
Wind shifts may be less visible, temperature may fall, and fog or mist can form.

00:00:11.256 --> 00:00:16.468
Humidity and condensation can affect lenses, sensors, electronics, and image quality.

00:00:16.468 --> 00:00:21.679
Cold night temperatures can reduce battery performance and usable flight time.

00:00:21.679 --> 00:00:29.391
Plan with larger reserves, follow approved battery-temperature guidance, and monitor actual consumption rather than assuming daylight endurance.

00:00:29.391 --> 00:00:35.853
Fog, mist, precipitation, and haze reduce visibility and can scatter aircraft or ground lighting.

00:00:35.853 --> 00:00:42.732
A light may remain visible even when the surrounding obstacles and flight path are not.

00:00:42.732 --> 00:00:48.360
Visibility must support the whole operation, not just sight of one beacon.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:54.822
Wires, trees, poles, towers, building edges, cranes, and vehicles can blend into dark backgrounds.

00:00:54.822 --> 00:00:59.617
Temporary obstacles and people may appear after the site review.

00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:03.994
Obstacle sensors may miss thin or poorly illuminated features.

00:01:03.994 --> 00:01:11.707
Launch and recovery areas need stable footing, clear boundaries, sufficient light, and control of people and vehicles.

00:01:11.707 --> 00:01:17.335
Protect crew night vision while making the landing surface and hazards identifiable.

00:01:17.335 --> 00:01:20.045
Prepare an alternate recovery area.

00:01:20.045 --> 00:01:22.755
Monitor changes throughout the flight.

00:01:22.755 --> 00:01:28.383
If temperature, wind, moisture, visibility, obstacles, or activity reduce margins, return early.

00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:36.096
A safe cancellation or shortened mission is better than discovering a night hazard during a low-battery landing.
